window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"p":"Quick start","h":"Quick start","u":"getting-started.html#quick-start","x":"A first photo in your own scene in about five minutes. No code."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"1. Add the camera","u":"getting-started.html#1-add-the-camera","x":"Open Tools > Photoworthy > Photo Studio . On the Start tab press Add Photo Camera . A Photo Camera appears in the scene, pointed at your game's main camera. Your camera is only borrowed: while a photo screen is open the kit zooms and moves it, and when the screen closes everything is put back exactly as it was."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"2. Pick how it plays","u":"getting-started.html#2-pick-how-it-plays","x":"Still on the Start tab, choose a Starting point (Wildlife Discovery is a good first one) and press Create Profile . A Photo Profile, a scoring asset and a catalog are made in Assets/RareGeometryData/Photoworthy and given to the camera. Every setting can be changed afterwards; nothing is locked."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"3. Make something photographable","u":"getting-started.html#3-make-something-photographable","x":"Select one or more objects in the Hierarchy (an animal, a statue, a door, a character) and press Make Selected Photographable , on the Start tab or in Tools > Photoworthy . Each object gets a Photo Subject component and a small subject asset named after it (\"SM_Red_Fox_01\" becomes \"Red Fox\"). Objects that share a subject asset count as the same kind of thing."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"4. Press Play","u":"getting-started.html#4-press-play","x":"Key What it does C Raise and put away the handheld camera Mouse wheel Zoom V Switch the camera body (SLR viewfinder, phone, camcorder, photo booth and more) F Flash: Auto, On, Off Z Swap the lens, when the profile lists more than one X Turn the camera round for a selfie, and back Space or left mouse button Take the photo Tab Album (G there raises the camera with that photo over the view, to take the same shot again) J Journal and requests P Photo mode O Options Esc Back With a gamepad (Input System installed): D-pad up raises the camera, RT takes the photo, LB and RB zoom, X switches the camer"},{"p":"Quick start","h":"5. Add a photo request (optional)","u":"getting-started.html#5-add-a-photo-request-optional","x":"Select a photographable object, open the Requests tab and press one of the buttons: Photograph It , A Good Shot , Doing Something , At A Place or Two Together . The request is added to the catalog and shows in the journal's Requests tab while playing. Take a photo that meets it and it completes."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"Something not working?","u":"getting-started.html#something-not-working","x":"Open Tools > Photoworthy > Check Scene For Problems . It lists what is missing or contradictory in plain words, most with a one-click fix. To see exactly why a view would score what it does, open Tools > Photoworthy > Score Inspector and press Judge Game Camera or Judge Scene View . It works before you press Play."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"Want to see everything working first?","u":"getting-started.html#want-to-see-everything-working-first","x":"Tools > Photoworthy > Open Demo > Meadow Trail is a small wildlife trail with animals, landmarks, six requests and a journal. Creator Lab has one station for each idea. See Demo scenes ."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Demo scenes","u":"samples.html#demo-scenes","x":"Two scenes, opened from Tools > Photoworthy > Open Demo . Both work in the Built-in, URP and HDRP pipelines and with either input system, with nothing to convert."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Controls","u":"samples.html#controls","x":"Key Gamepad What it does W A S D, mouse Left stick, right stick Walk and look C D-pad up Raise and put away the camera Mouse wheel LB, RB Zoom Space or left mouse button RT Take the photo Tab D-pad left Album J D-pad right Journal and requests P D-pad down Photo mode O Start, in a menu Options L Swap the look of every screen (demo only) Esc B Back Gamepad needs the Input System package."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Meadow Trail","u":"samples.html#meadow-trail","x":"A short wildlife trail: the game the Wildlife Discovery starting point makes. Nine animals of seven kinds. The horse, zebras and cow wander, run and jump; the others stand and hop. Each tells its Photo Subject what it is doing, so photos know. Six landmarks : the Great Oak, the Old Obelisk, the Stone Head, the Stone Ring, the Trail Camp (one subject made of several objects, with its own points) and the Old Canoe. The pond is a Photo Region, used by a request. Six requests , each showing a different kind of rule: any subject, a good portrait facing the camera, any animal mid-jump, two different"},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Creator Lab","u":"samples.html#creator-lab","x":"A fenced yard with five places to stand. Step on a violet disc and a notice says what to try. Any object can be a subject. The column was made photographable with one button. Step sideways for a clear view. A wall hides most of the stone head. The result says \"Partly hidden\"; one step to the side and it is clear. This is the request \"A Clear View\". Catch the jump. The pug jumps often. Catch it in the air for the request \"The Right Moment\". Two subjects in one photo. The sheep and the llama, for the request \"A Pair\". Press P for photo mode. A pretty corner to fly around."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"How the demos are made","u":"samples.html#how-the-demos-are-made","x":"Samples/Scripts holds the small scripts the demos use: a first-person walker ( DemoWalker ), an animal that wanders and reports its behaviour ( DemoAnimal , a good example of the one line your own AI needs), surfaces and lights that follow the render pipeline, the look button and the station notices. They are examples, not part of the kit: your game keeps its own controller. Samples/Data holds the subjects, requests, catalogs, profiles and looks. Open them to see how each is set up. Samples/Models holds CC0 models by Quaternius and Kenney. See Third-Party Notices.txt . The whole Samples folder"},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Demo photos","u":"samples.html#demo-photos","x":"Photos taken in the demos are saved under the project's persistent data path, in Photos/Meadow Trail Demo and Photos/Creator Lab Demo . Delete those folders to start again, or delete photos in the album."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Fit it into your game","u":"fit-your-game.html#fit-it-into-your-game","x":"A photo is an event. The kit captures it, recognises what is in it, judges it, responds and presents it, and every one of those five steps can be switched off or replaced. This page shows how to bend it to your game."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"The five steps","u":"fit-your-game.html#the-five-steps","x":"Step What happens Switch it off Replace it Capture The shutter fires; the moment becomes data and, usually, a picture. Storage: Make Pictures off. Take photos from your own code with Capture or PhotoShoot.Take . Recognise The camera works out which subjects are in the picture. Recognition: Off. Mark objects photographable or not at any time. Evaluate The photo is judged. Scoring mode Off or Identify Only. A custom scorer, or change the result in PhotoEvaluated . Respond Requests complete, the journal fills, your events run, the photo is kept or not. Requests off, Journal off, Album off. Decide"},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Common setups","u":"fit-your-game.html#common-setups","x":"A quest that needs a photo, and nothing else. Starting point Photo Quests. Make the quest object photographable, add a request with \"Photograph It\", and wire a Photo Trigger (Request Completed) to your quest system. Photos as evidence in a stealth game. Starting point Stealth Evidence. Film is limited, photos pass or fail. Add details to subjects (a face, a document) and ask for them with the Shows Detail rule. The camera as a weapon. Starting point Ghost Combat. Nothing is kept. Listen to PhotoEvaluated , or put a Photo Trigger on each ghost with When set to This Object Photographed and use t"},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Events with no code","u":"fit-your-game.html#events-with-no-code","x":"The Photo Camera has UnityEvents for Opened, Closed, Photo Taken, Photo Kept, Photo Discarded, Photo Saved and Shot Refused. A Photo Subject has On Photographed. A Photo Trigger (Add Component > Photoworthy > Photo Trigger) reacts to photos anywhere in the scene: When Fires for Any Photo Kept Every kept photo. This Object Photographed A kept photo that shows this object's own Photo Subject. Subject Photographed A kept photo that shows a kind of subject. Request Completed A request being completed. Subject Discovered A subject photographed for the first time. Rules Met A kept photo that meets r"},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Events in code","u":"fit-your-game.html#events-in-code","x":"PhotoEvents raises every step, in this order: Event When CameraOpened, CameraClosed A camera screen opens or closes. CaptureRequested The shutter fired. Nothing is judged or saved yet. PhotoEvaluated Recognised and judged. Change the photo here to overrule the result. PhotoAccepted or PhotoRejected Kept, or not. SubjectDiscovered, BehaviourDiscovered, NewBestShot, RequestCompleted What the kept photo changed. PhotoSaved The picture file is safely on disk."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Your own camera","u":"fit-your-game.html#your-own-camera","x":"The Photo Camera borrows whatever camera you give it, so it works with any controller, including Cinemachine: give it the Unity Camera that renders the game. While a screen is open the kit sets the field of view and, in the photo mode, the position; list your controller under Pause In Photo Mode so the two do not fight. In a third-person game, set Handheld Viewpoint to a transform at the character's eyes and the handheld camera looks from there. For a camera the player never holds (a security camera, a drone, a finish-line photo): PhotoShoot.Take(securityCamera, profile, PhotoSource.Script);"},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Your own input","u":"fit-your-game.html#your-own-input","x":"Switch Built In Input off in the profile's Keys and call the camera yourself: photoCamera.ToggleHandheld(); // or OpenHandheld(), OpenPhotoMode(), Close() photoCamera.TakePhoto(); photoCamera.Zoom = 0.5f; // 0 is the widest view, 1 the closest To keep the built-in screens' navigation but feed it from your own input system, write an IPhotoInput and hand it over with PhotoInput.Use(myInput) ."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Your own UI","u":"fit-your-game.html#your-own-ui","x":"Switch the built-in screens off (all, or only the ones you replace) and draw from the events and data: photoCamera.ShotReady += shot => { scoreLabel.text = shot.Photo.judged ? Mathf.RoundToInt(shot.Photo.score).ToString() : \"\"; preview.texture = shot.Texture; }; The album and journal are plain data ( PhotoAlbum.Main.Photos , PhotoJournal.Main.Entries ). Mix and match: keep the built-in viewfinder and result card, and show the album in your own menu."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Your own rule for what is kept","u":"fit-your-game.html#your-own-rule-for-what-is-kept","x":"photoCamera.DecideKeep = shot => shot.Photo.Shows(\"ghost\"); // true keeps, false discards, null lets the profile decide"},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Your own data on photos","u":"fit-your-game.html#your-own-data-on-photos","x":"Stamp every photo with values from your game and use them in rules (Data Equals) and your own UI: photoCamera.SetStamp(\"weather\", \"storm\"); photoCamera.SetStamp(\"chapter\", \"3\"); Subjects carry their own values the same way (the Data list on a Photo Subject, or SetData )."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Saving","u":"fit-your-game.html#saving","x":"Photos are files; the album looks after them. Use a different Storage Folder per save slot. The journal saves itself, joins a save product's slots when one is installed, or goes into your own saves with ToJson and FromJson . See Album and journal . Players' options are remembered per device."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Pausing and time","u":"fit-your-game.html#pausing-and-time","x":"The handheld camera keeps the game running by default; the photo mode pauses it. Each can pause, slow or keep the world running (the World setting). The kit stacks its changes on top of whatever time scale your game had and puts it back exactly, so your own slow motion survives."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Working with other Rare Geometry products","u":"fit-your-game.html#working-with-other-rare-geometry-products","x":"Each link switches on by itself when the other product is in the project, and does nothing when it is not. There is nothing to install and no setup. With What you get A map product (Bearing) Tick Show On Maps on a Photo Subject and it appears on the compass, minimap and map: undiscovered until photographed, the objective while an open request asks for it. A save product (Shipready) The journal is part of the game's save slots. The players' shared accessibility choices (text size, reduced motion, high contrast, flash) apply to the camera screens. An interaction product (Interactra) Add Subject "},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Multiplayer","u":"fit-your-game.html#multiplayer","x":"Photos are local to each player: the camera, album and journal belong to the machine they run on. To share a result, send what you need from the photo record (subject, score) over your own network code."},{"p":"Fit it into your game","h":"Platforms","u":"fit-your-game.html#platforms","x":"Tested on Windows. Nothing in the kit is tied to a platform: it uses only Unity's own rendering, file and UI features. Saving a copy to the Pictures folder is offered on desktop only. On WebGL, where there are no threads, pictures are made on the main thread."},{"p":"Subjects","h":"Subjects","u":"subjects.html#subjects","x":"A subject is anything a photo can be about: an animal, a person, a landmark, a clue, a whole camp. Two pieces make one: A subject asset says what kind of thing it is: its name, journal section, behaviours and tags. Many objects can share one (\"Zebra\"). A Photo Subject component on an object in the scene says \"this is one of those\"."},{"p":"Subjects","h":"Make one","u":"subjects.html#make-one","x":"Select objects and press Tools > Photoworthy > Make Selected Photographable . Each gets a Photo Subject and, when needed, a new subject asset named after it in Assets/RareGeometryData/Photoworthy/Subjects . To make several objects the same kind, give them the same subject asset."},{"p":"Subjects","h":"The subject asset","u":"subjects.html#the-subject-asset","x":"Setting What it does Id The key saved in photos and the journal. Keep it once players have saves. Display Name The name players read. Description A line or two for the journal page. Category The journal section it is listed under (Animals, Landmarks, Clues). Icon, Color Shown in the journal and offered to maps. Behaviours What it can be seen doing. Each has a key (\"jump\"), a name (\"Jumping\"), a rarity tier from 1 to 4 and an action score. Tags Free words such as \"bird\" or \"nocturnal\", for request rules. Scoring Override A different scoring asset for this kind of subject (a landmark is not judg"},{"p":"Subjects","h":"The Photo Subject component","u":"subjects.html#the-photo-subject-component","x":"Setting What it does Definition The subject asset. Photographable Switch off to hide the object from the camera for a while (a ghost that is not there yet). Anchor Mode Automatic checks 15 points over the object's bounds. Custom uses your own points, for a subject made of several things or with an odd shape. Details Named parts of the subject that a photo can show: a face, a licence plate, a wound. Each is a point, optionally only counted when it faces the camera. Front, Has Front Which way it faces. Switch Has Front off for a tree or a lake so facing is never judged. Max Distance The farthest"},{"p":"Subjects","h":"Behaviours","u":"subjects.html#behaviours","x":"Tell a subject what it is doing and photos know. One line from your own animation or AI code: GetComponent<PhotoSubject>().SetBehaviour(\"jump\"); Or with no code: call SetBehaviour , BeginBehaviour or EndBehaviour from an Animation Event or any UnityEvent. A subject can be doing several things at once (walking and eating). With nothing set, the first behaviour of the subject asset counts. The journal lists each behaviour once photographed, and a photo of a rarer behaviour scores more for Action."},{"p":"Subjects","h":"How the camera recognises a subject","u":"subjects.html#how-the-camera-recognises-a-subject","x":"At the moment of the shutter, on the CPU, with no extra rendering: Subjects too far away, switched off or behind the camera are skipped. Up to 15 points on the subject are projected into the picture. The share inside the frame is how much is in frame ; the box round them is how much of the picture it fills . A line is cast from the lens to each point. Points behind a wall or another object are hidden . The subject's own colliders never hide it. Choose which layers can hide things in the profile (leave out glass and leaves). The angle between the subject's front and the camera gives facing . A "},{"p":"Subjects","h":"Places","u":"subjects.html#places","x":"A Photo Region (GameObject > Photoworthy > Photo Region) is a trigger volume with a key and a name. A photo knows which regions the camera stood in and which regions each subject was in, so requests can ask for \"a zebra at the pond\" or \"taken from the tower\"."},{"p":"Subjects","h":"Scene gizmos","u":"subjects.html#scene-gizmos","x":"Select a Photo Subject to see its points, front arrow and maximum distance in the Scene view."},{"p":"Scoring","h":"Scoring","u":"scoring.html#scoring","x":"Judging photos is optional. When it is on, every point has a reason a player can read."},{"p":"Scoring","h":"Scoring modes","u":"scoring.html#scoring-modes","x":"A Scoring Profile asset (Create > Photoworthy) has a mode: Mode What a photo gets Off Nothing. Photos are never judged. Identify Only What it shows. No points, no medals. Pass Or Fail Good enough or not, against a pass mark. Points A score from 0 to the top score, with a medal. Custom Your own scorer asset decides."},{"p":"Scoring","h":"The six criteria","u":"scoring.html#the-six-criteria","x":"Criterion Question it answers Default weight Framing Is the subject where it should be in the picture (centre, or on a thirds line)? 25 Size Does it fill the right share of the picture? 20 Visibility How much of it can be seen, not hidden or cut off? 20 Facing Does it face the camera? 15 Action How rare is what it is doing? 15 Context What else is in the picture: other subjects, special places. 5 Each criterion can be switched off; the others then share the points, so the top score stays the same. A subject with no front is never judged on Facing."},{"p":"Scoring","h":"The main subject","u":"scoring.html#the-main-subject","x":"A photo can show several subjects, and each gets its own score sheet. The photo's own score is its main subject's : the one under the centre mark (the nearest, when several are), or otherwise the largest one near the middle. So a far-off landmark never takes a photo away from the animal the player aimed at. Requests about a particular subject always use that subject's own score."},{"p":"Scoring","h":"Medals and rarity","u":"scoring.html#medals-and-rarity","x":"With points, a photo earns Bronze for any counted subject, Silver from 50% of the top score, Gold from 70% and Diamond from 90%. The marks are settings. Quality and rarity are kept apart on purpose. The medal says how good the photo is. The tier (1 to 4) says how rare the moment was. A plain photo of a rare behaviour and a perfect photo of a common one are both worth showing in the journal, in different ways."},{"p":"Scoring","h":"Reasons","u":"scoring.html#reasons","x":"Every result carries plain reasons: what held it back (\"Too small\", \"Facing away\", \"Partly hidden\", \"Cut off\", \"Off centre\") and what went well (\"Well framed\", \"Clear view\", \"Rare moment\"). The result card shows the most important ones. Switch them off with Show Hints in the profile's Review settings. All the words can be changed; see Screens and looks ."},{"p":"Scoring","h":"Presets","u":"scoring.html#presets","x":"Pick one in the Scoring Profile's Inspector, then adjust. Preset For Wildlife The six criteria in balance: animals and creatures. Portrait People and characters: facing and thirds framing matter most. Landmark Buildings and views: framing, size and a clear view. No front, no action. Small Object Small things: getting close and a clear view. Group Shot Several subjects together: company counts. Action Moment The right moment: what the subject is doing matters most. Identify Only No points. Pass Or Fail Good enough or not. A subject asset can carry its own scoring asset (Scoring Override), so a "},{"p":"Scoring","h":"The Score Inspector","u":"scoring.html#the-score-inspector","x":"Tools > Photoworthy > Score Inspector answers \"why did this photo get that score?\" without guessing. Judge Game Camera judges what the Photo Camera's game camera sees right now. Judge Scene View judges what the Scene view sees: frame a subject in the Scene view and press it. For each subject it lists every criterion with its points and reason, the numbers behind them (share of the picture, share visible, facing, distance) and the points checked. Nearby but not counted lists subjects that were close to counting, with the reason each did not. It works in Edit Mode, so you can tune a scoring asse"},{"p":"Scoring","h":"Your own scorer","u":"scoring.html#your-own-scorer","x":"For rules no setting covers, make an asset class and set the Scoring Profile's mode to Custom: using System.Collections.Generic; using RareGeometry.Photoworthy; using UnityEngine; [CreateAssetMenu(menuName = \"My Game/Night Scorer\")] public class NightScorer : CustomPhotoScorer { public override void Evaluate(PhotoContext context, IReadOnlyList<SubjectSighting> sightings, ScoringProfile profile, PhotoEvaluation result) { // One score per subject in the picture; Finish picks the best as the photo's score and hands out the medal. foreach (var sighting in sightings) { var night = context.GameTime "},{"p":"Photo requests","h":"Photo requests","u":"requests.html#photo-requests","x":"A photo request is a quest for a photo: \"photograph the horse\", \"catch any animal mid-jump\", \"a zebra at the pond\". Requests are assets built from rules, with no code. They are optional: photos work without them."},{"p":"Photo requests","h":"Make one","u":"requests.html#make-one","x":"Select a photographable object, open Tools > Photoworthy > Photo Studio , the Requests tab, and press a template: Photograph It , A Good Shot , Doing Something , At A Place or Two Together . The new request is added to the catalog and selected so you can change it. Or make one by hand: Create > Photoworthy > Photo Request , then add it to the Photo Catalog's Requests list."},{"p":"Photo requests","h":"The request asset","u":"requests.html#the-request-asset","x":"Setting What it does Id The key saved in the journal. Keep it once players have saves. Title, Description What players read in the journal's Requests tab. Reward A line of words shown with the request. Hand out the real reward from the completed event. Rule lists One or more lists of rules. Inside a list, choose All (every rule) or Any (at least one). Between lists, choose All or Any again. That gives AND and OR with no code. Offered From Start On offer as soon as the game starts. Off waits for your code or an event to offer it. Repeatable Can be completed again and again. Show Rules Lists the"},{"p":"Photo requests","h":"The 18 rules","u":"requests.html#the-18-rules","x":"Rule Passes when Shows Subject The photo shows the subject (at least Count of them). With no subject chosen: any subject. Shows Category It shows subjects from a journal section, such as Animals. Shows Tag It shows subjects with a tag, such as \"bird\". Subject Is Doing A subject is doing something (the behaviour key). With no subject chosen: any subject. Shows Detail A named detail of a subject shows (a face, a plate). Different Subjects Together At least Count different kinds of subject are in the photo. Score At Least The score reaches a share of the top score. Medal At Least The photo earned"},{"p":"Photo requests","h":"What players see","u":"requests.html#what-players-see","x":"The journal's Requests tab lists each request on offer with its title, description, reward and, when Show Rules is on, its rules. When a kept photo meets a request, it completes: a notice shows, the journal marks it done, and RequestCompleted is raised. The result card lists the requests a photo completed."},{"p":"Photo requests","h":"Offer and withdraw","u":"requests.html#offer-and-withdraw","x":"Requests marked Offered From Start in the camera's catalog are on offer at once. For the rest: PhotoRequests.Offer(request); // put it on offer (from a quest giver, a story beat) PhotoRequests.Withdraw(request); // take it off again Both can be called from a UnityEvent through a small script of your own, or from a Photo Trigger."},{"p":"Photo requests","h":"React to a completed request","u":"requests.html#react-to-a-completed-request","x":"With no code: add a Photo Trigger component, set When to Request Completed , pick the request, and wire its event (open a gate, give a reward, start a dialogue). In code: PhotoEvents.RequestCompleted += (request, photo) => Debug.Log(request.Title + \" done with \" + photo.id);"},{"p":"Photo requests","h":"Your own rule","u":"requests.html#your-own-rule","x":"using RareGeometry.Photoworthy; using UnityEngine; [CreateAssetMenu(menuName = \"My Game/Rule: At Night\")] public class AtNightRule : CustomPhotoRule { public override bool Passes(Photo photo, PhotoContext context) => photo.GetData(\"time\") == \"night\"; public override string Describe() => \"Taken at night\"; } Set a rule's kind to Custom and give it the asset. Stamp your own values on photos with the Photo Camera's SetStamp(\"time\", \"night\") . A selfie carries the value selfie = yes by itself, so \"a selfie with the llama\" is two rules: Shows Subject (Llama) and Data Equals ( selfie , yes )."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Cameras","u":"cameras.html#cameras","x":"One Photo Camera component gives players two ways to take photos. Use either, both or neither (your own code can take photos with no camera screen at all)."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"The Photo Camera component","u":"cameras.html#the-photo-camera-component","x":"Setting What it does Game Camera The camera your game renders with. Empty finds the one tagged MainCamera. Profile The Photo Profile: how photography works in your game. Empty uses a built-in profile with everything on. Handheld Viewpoint Where the handheld camera looks from, if not the game camera's own place (a third-person game puts it at the character's eyes). Pause While Open Components switched off while any camera screen is open (your shooting script). Pause In Photo Mode Components switched off while the photo mode is open (your player controller). Photo Mode Focus The character or obj"},{"p":"Cameras","h":"The handheld camera","u":"cameras.html#the-handheld-camera","x":"The in-world camera, used while the game runs: raise it, zoom, take the photo. Settings are in the profile under Handheld. Setting What it does Enabled Players can raise it. Widest View, Closest Zoom The zoom range, as fields of view. Zoom Speed, Zoom Smoothing How zooming feels. Controls Look Let the kit turn the view while the camera is up. Leave off when your own controller keeps looking around. Look Speed, Steady Aim, Slow Look When Zoomed How aiming feels, with a hold-to-steady button for long lenses. World What the game does while it is up: Keep Running, Pause or Slow Motion. Counts For "},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Camera bodies","u":"cameras.html#camera-bodies","x":"A camera body is a camera the player looks through: the handheld camera then looks like a real camera held up in front of them, not a bare screen. Eight are built in, and players switch between them with V while the camera is up: Body What the player sees SLR Viewfinder Through the eyepiece: a dark surround, focus points, a readout strip with the lens length and film left. Camera Back The back of a camera held up: its screen, a dial, a four-way pad and buttons. Phone A phone held sideways with its camera app: a shutter button, a zoom readout. Camcorder A fold-out screen with REC, a running clo"},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Make your own","u":"cameras.html#make-your-own","x":"Create > Photoworthy > Camera Body makes a body asset. Pick a Style, press Use This Style's Colours And Window , then change anything: Setting What it does Style How the body is drawn: one of the seven above, or Picture for your own artwork. Window How much of the screen's height the picture window takes. Own Shape, Shape The picture shape of this body. Off uses the profile's. Corner How round the window's corners are. Zoom Limit How much of the profile's zoom range this body can use. 0 is a fixed lens. Body, Trim, Accent, Text The body's colours. Outside How dark the world outside a camera he"},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Lenses","u":"cameras.html#lenses","x":"List lenses in the profile under Handheld > Lenses and players swap between them with Z while the camera is up: a wide lens for landscapes, a standard one, a long telephoto for shy animals. Each lens is a name and a zoom range (Widest View and Closest Zoom, as fields of view), and a new lens always starts at its widest. With no lenses listed the camera has one lens: the zoom range at the top of Handheld. Untick Unlocked on a lens and it becomes a reward: the player does not have it until your game calls UnlockLens(\"Telephoto\") on the Photo Camera, for a finished request, a shop, a level. LockL"},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Selfies","u":"cameras.html#selfies","x":"X turns the handheld camera round (Start on a gamepad). It is held at arm's length in front of the player and looks back at them, with whatever is behind them in the picture. X again turns it back. Everything else works as before: the camera bodies, the flash, bursts, the photo booth strip. The face. Give the Photo Camera a Selfie Focus : a transform at the character's face or head. Without one the camera uses the Photo Mode Focus, then the Handheld Viewpoint, then the game camera's own place. A first-person game. The character model is usually hidden. List it under Shown In Selfie and it is s"},{"p":"Cameras","h":"The flash","u":"cameras.html#the-flash","x":"The flash is a real light, not a white screen: it fires at the shutter, so it is in the saved picture, and it lights whatever your own lights light. Players switch it between Auto, On and Off with F while the camera is up, and the viewfinder says which it is. Settings are in the profile under Handheld > Flash. Setting What it does Enabled The camera has a flash. Mode When it fires, until the player chooses: Off, Auto (when the view is dark) or On. Players Switch Players can switch the mode while the camera is up. Brightness, Brightness Hdrp How bright it is. The High Definition pipeline measur"},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Photo strips","u":"cameras.html#photo-strips","x":"A photo strip is several photos printed as one tall picture, the way a photo booth does it. The Photo Booth body takes one with every press; from code, any camera can: TakeStrip(4, 3f) takes four photos with a three second countdown before each. Every photo of a strip is an ordinary photo (judged, kept and counted like any other); the strip is an extra picture in the album. The shutter button or Back stops it part way. How it is printed is in the profile under Strip: the paper's colour, the border, the gap between photos, the wider band at the bottom, how tall each photo is, and whether the st"},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Focus lock","u":"cameras.html#focus-lock","x":"With subject marks on, a bracket glides to whichever subject is under the centre mark and closes in with a chirp when it has held it for a moment. It tells the player \"this is what the photo will be of\" before they press the shutter. Switch it off under Screens > Focus Lock. From code: FocusTarget and the FocusLocked event."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Match a shot","u":"cameras.html#match-a-shot","x":"In the album, G raises the camera with the chosen photo shown faintly over the view, so the player can take the same shot again: a before-and-after, a \"find where this was taken\" quest. G again takes it away, and so does putting the camera down. From code: ShowGhost(photo) , ClearGhost() , GhostOpacity ."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"The photo mode","u":"cameras.html#the-photo-mode","x":"A free camera for beautiful pictures: fly, zoom, tilt, set the lens, hide the controls, take the photo. Settings are in the profile under Photo Mode. Setting What it does Enabled Players can open it. World Pause (the default), Slow Motion or Keep Running. Move Speed, Fast Multiplier, Look Speed, Smoothing How flying feels. Travel Radius How far the camera can fly from where it started. 0 is no limit. Collide, Collision Radius, Collision Layers Stop the camera at walls and floors. Widest View, Closest Zoom, Max Tilt The lens and tilt limits. Counts For Progress Off by default, so players cannot"},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Auto focus","u":"cameras.html#auto-focus","x":"With focus blur on, the focus follows whatever is under the centre mark: a photo subject first (it needs no collider), otherwise the first thing a ray from the lens hits. Changing the focus distance by hand takes over until auto focus is switched on again."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Orbit","u":"cameras.html#orbit","x":"Give the Photo Camera a Photo Mode Focus (a transform at the character's chest or head) and the photo mode gets a Camera row: Free flies as before, Orbit circles the focus. In Orbit, looking turns the camera round the focus, forward and back move in and out, and up and down raise the point being circled. Walls still stop the camera."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Viewpoints","u":"cameras.html#viewpoints","x":"A Photo Spot (Add Component > Photoworthy > Photo Spot) is a camera position you place in the scene: point its blue arrow where the camera should look and give it a name. The photo mode's Viewpoint row steps through them, so players can jump to the best views you found. From a spot they can still look around and fly on. A spot is also a fixed camera for your game: call its Capture() from any event (a ride passing a finish line, a security camera, a checkpoint) and a photo is taken from there, with the game camera put straight back."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"The shutter timer","u":"cameras.html#the-shutter-timer","x":"The Timer row sets 3, 5 or 10 seconds. The shutter then counts down in the middle of the view before the photo is taken, so players can pose or hide the controls first. Pressing the shutter or Back again cancels it. From code: TakePhotoAfter(seconds) and CancelTimer() ."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Lens effects in every pipeline","u":"cameras.html#lens-effects-in-every-pipeline","x":"Focus blur, brightness, contrast, colour, warmth, tint and dark corners work the same in all three render pipelines, with nothing to set up: Built-in : the kit's own image effect. No Post Processing package needed. URP and HDRP : the kit drives a temporary Volume while a camera screen is open, and removes it on closing. Post processing must be on for the camera, which is the default in new projects. The saved picture has the same look as the screen."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Filters and frames","u":"cameras.html#filters-and-frames","x":"Seven filters (Warm, Cool, Vivid, Faded, Black and White, Sepia, Noir) and four frames (White border, Instant print, Black border, Film bars) are built in. Make your own with Create > Photoworthy > Filter and Frame , and list them in the profile's Filters and Frames. A frame with the Picture style uses your own artwork. Frames are drawn into the saved picture."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Film and pace","u":"cameras.html#film-and-pace","x":"Under Film in the profile: Setting What it does Limited Photos use up film. Off means players can shoot for ever. Starting Shots, Max Shots How much film the camera starts with and can hold. Give more with AddFilm . Cooldown Seconds before the next photo. Burst, Burst Interval How many photos one press of the handheld shutter takes, and the gap between them. A burst of 3 to 5 is the easy way to catch a jump. Each photo uses film. Burst Sheet Print each burst as one contact sheet as well: its photos side by side on one picture in the album. Refund Rejected Photos that are not kept give their sh"},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Which photos are kept","u":"cameras.html#which-photos-are-kept","x":"Under Review in the profile, Keep decides: Everything, Only With A Subject, Only Good Shots, or Player Chooses (the result card waits for Keep or Discard). For your own rule, set the camera's DecideKeep in code. See Scripting ."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"When the camera says no","u":"cameras.html#when-the-camera-says-no","x":"Block the camera from your game (a cutscene, a no-photo zone) with Blocked = true , or give it a rule with CanOpen and CanShoot . When a photo is refused, the Shot Refused event says why: no film, album full, not ready, or blocked."},{"p":"Cameras","h":"Keys","u":"cameras.html#keys","x":"All keys are in the profile under Keys, with gamepad buttons added when the Input System is installed. Switch Built In Input off to drive everything from your own input: call OpenHandheld , OpenPhotoMode , TakePhoto and Close . See Fit it into your game ."},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"Album and journal","u":"album-and-journal.html#album-and-journal","x":"Two records, both optional. The album is the photos themselves. The journal is what the player has found."},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"The album","u":"album-and-journal.html#the-album","x":"Every kept photo becomes a real picture file plus a small record of what it shows."},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"What is saved","u":"album-and-journal.html#what-is-saved","x":"Under Storage in the profile: Setting What it does Make Pictures Off keeps only the record of what was photographed, with no image at all. Album Off means a photo only makes things happen (requests, discoveries, your events) and is not kept. Save To Disk Off keeps the album only until the game closes. Journal Fill in the journal. Folder The folder under the game's save location. Use a different one per save slot or player. Max Photos, When Full The most photos the album holds, and whether the oldest makes room or new photos are refused. Favourites and best shots are never replaced. Format, Jpg"},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"The album screen","u":"album-and-journal.html#the-album-screen","x":"Tab opens it. Tabs for All, Favourites and Best shots; a grid of pictures and a detail pane with the photo, what it shows, its score and medal. F marks a favourite, Delete removes a photo (with a confirm), Enter saves a copy to the player's Pictures folder on desktop platforms, and G raises the camera with that photo shown faintly over the view, to take the same shot again. Photo strips from the photo booth are in the album too."},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"From code","u":"album-and-journal.html#from-code","x":"var album = PhotoAlbum.Main; foreach (var photo in album.Photos) Debug.Log(photo.mainSubject + \" \" + photo.score); album.LoadPicture(album.Photos[0], texture => myImage.texture = texture); For a different place to keep photos (cloud saves, a database), write an IPhotoStore and open the album on it with PhotoAlbum.Open(myStore) . MemoryPhotoStore keeps everything in memory."},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"Photos in the world","u":"album-and-journal.html#photos-in-the-world","x":"A Photo Display (Add Component > Photoworthy > Photo Display) shows one of the player's photos on an object: a picture frame on a wall, a pin board, a trophy case, a phone screen. Put it on anything with a renderer (a Quad works well) and choose what it shows: the latest photo, the best photo of a subject, the latest favourite, or the latest photo of a subject. It follows the album, so the frame changes when a better photo is taken, and it can stretch the object to the photo's own shape. The Meadow Trail demo has one on a board by the trail head."},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"The journal","u":"album-and-journal.html#the-journal","x":"The journal remembers, for each kind of subject: when it was first photographed, how many photos, the best shot and score, and which behaviours have been seen, each with its own best shot. It also remembers which requests are done."},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"The journal screen","u":"album-and-journal.html#the-journal-screen","x":"J opens it. One tab per section (Animals, Landmarks, your own categories) plus Requests. Subjects not found yet show as \"?\" when Secret Until Found is on. A found subject shows its best photo, description, score and medal, and its behaviours: seen ones by name, the rest as \"Not seen yet\". The order of sections and which subjects are listed come from the Photo Catalog asset (Create > Photoworthy > Catalog) on the profile."},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"Saving","u":"album-and-journal.html#saving","x":"By itself, the journal saves to a small file next to the photos, whenever it changes. With a save product that reads the shared save contract (Shipready does), the journal is part of the game's save slots automatically. For your own save system: PhotoJournal.Main.ToJson() gives the whole journal as text, and FromJson reads it back."},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"From code","u":"album-and-journal.html#from-code_1","x":"var journal = PhotoJournal.Main; if (journal.IsDiscovered(\"zebra\")) Debug.Log(\"Best zebra: \" + journal.Entry(\"zebra\").bestScore);"},{"p":"Album and journal","h":"Starting again","u":"album-and-journal.html#starting-again","x":"PhotoAlbum.Main.Clear() deletes every photo and PhotoJournal.Main.Clear() empties the journal, for a New Game button."},{"p":"Screens and looks","h":"Screens and looks","u":"screens.html#screens-and-looks","x":"Everything players see is built in, works with keyboard, mouse and gamepad, and can be restyled, reworded or switched off piece by piece."},{"p":"Screens and looks","h":"The screens","u":"screens.html#the-screens","x":"Screen When What it shows Viewfinder While a camera is up Corner brackets, a centre mark, optional guide lines (thirds, golden ratio, diagonals or centre), the zoom, film left, the flash, the current request, the shutter timer's countdown, a mark on each subject the camera would recognise and a focus bracket that locks on to the one under the centre mark. A camera body, when one is in use, is drawn round all of it. When photos are a different shape from the screen, the part that will be cut off is dimmed. Result card After a shot The photo, what it shows, the score and medal, the points for ea"},{"p":"Screens and looks","h":"After the shot: how a photo is shown","u":"screens.html#after-the-shot-how-a-photo-is-shown","x":"The result after a shot can look six ways. Pick one in the profile under Review > Style : Style What players see Card A card at the side of the screen: the photo as a print with everything about it underneath. The default. Print A sheet of photo paper in the middle of the screen, a little tilted, as if handed to the player. Full Screen The photo fills the screen, the way a camera plays a picture back, with a small card in the corner. Camera Screen The photo plays back on the camera body's own screen, inside its window. With no camera body it fills the screen. Thumbnail A small picture in a cor"},{"p":"Screens and looks","h":"Looks","u":"screens.html#looks","x":"A Photo Look asset holds every colour, corner, line thickness, the font, text size, title capitals and motion time. Three are built in: Field Journal : warm paper, ink text, a rust accent, photos as prints. Modern Viewfinder : dark glass, light text, an amber accent. Instant Print : white cards, round corners, a coral accent, thick print borders. Make your own with Create > Photoworthy > Look , start from one of the three in its Inspector, change anything, and give it to the profile. A look can be swapped while the game runs: photoCamera.GetComponent<PhotoScreens>().LookOverride = nightLook; I"},{"p":"Screens and looks","h":"Words","u":"screens.html#words","x":"Every word players read comes from one table, so the screens can be reworded or translated without touching code. Create > Photoworthy > Text Table , add rows (a key and your words), and give it to the profile. Rows you leave out keep the built-in English. Add Every Key in the table's Inspector adds a row for every key with its English words, ready to reword or translate. For a localisation system, set PhotoText.Translate to a function that receives a key and the English words and returns yours. From code: PhotoText.Set(\"result.newDiscovery\", \"First sighting!\") . Subject names, descriptions, b"},{"p":"Screens and looks","h":"Players' options","u":"screens.html#players-options","x":"The options screen lets players set things for themselves. You choose what is offered. Group Options Camera Look speed, invert look Screen Guide lines, subject marks, key hints, shutter flash Sound Camera sounds volume Accessibility Text size, reduced motion, high contrast With nothing set up, every option is offered. To decide yourself, Create > Photoworthy > Player Options : Setting What it does Defaults What every option starts at, and what Reset goes back to. Limits How far the look speed and text size sliders move. Camera, Screen, Sound, Accessibility Options Which groups the screen offer"},{"p":"Screens and looks","h":"Accessibility","u":"screens.html#accessibility","x":"Text size scales every word on every screen. Reduced motion makes panels and cards appear at once. High contrast draws solid panels, plain black or white text and thicker lines, whatever the look. Shutter flash can be switched off. Every screen works without a mouse: arrows or D-pad to move, Enter or A to press, Esc or B to back out. Results are never told by colour alone: medals have names, reasons are words. These also follow the shared accessibility settings that every Rare Geometry product reads, so a settings product (Shipready) or your own code can set them once for the whole game: RareG"},{"p":"Screens and looks","h":"Sounds","u":"screens.html#sounds","x":"The shutter, a chime for discoveries, a blip when the camera says no and a tick in menus are made by the kit, with no audio files. Replace any of them with your own clip, and set the volume, in the profile under Sounds."},{"p":"Screens and looks","h":"Your own panel settings","u":"screens.html#your-own-panel-settings","x":"The Photo Screens component (added to the camera automatically) has a Sorting Order, so the screens draw above or below your other UI Toolkit panels, and a slot for your own Panel Settings."},{"p":"Scripting","h":"Scripting","u":"scripting.html#scripting","x":"Everything the built-in screens do goes through public code you can call yourself. All types are in the RareGeometry.Photoworthy namespace; reference the RareGeometry.Photoworthy assembly from your own assembly definition if you use one."},{"p":"Scripting","h":"The camera","u":"scripting.html#the-camera","x":"using RareGeometry.Photoworthy; using UnityEngine; public class MyCameraButtons : MonoBehaviour { public PhotoCamera photoCamera; // or PhotoCamera.Main void Update() { if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.F)) photoCamera.ToggleHandheld(); if (photoCamera.IsOpen && Input.GetMouseButtonDown(0)) { var shot = photoCamera.TakePhoto(); if (shot == null) Debug.Log(\"Refused: \" + photoCamera.WhyNot()); } } } Member What it does OpenHandheld() , OpenPhotoMode() , Close() , ToggleHandheld() , TogglePhotoMode() Open and close the camera screens. TakePhoto() A photo through the open screen. Returns the shot, or n"},{"p":"Scripting","h":"A shot and a photo","u":"scripting.html#a-shot-and-a-photo","x":"TakePhoto returns a PhotoShot at once. Its picture arrives a moment later ( Ready , or the ShotReady event), because reading pixels back and writing the file do not block the game. photoCamera.ShotDecided += shot => { if (!shot.Kept) return; Photo photo = shot.Photo; Debug.Log(photo.mainSubject + \": \" + photo.score + \" (\" + photo.medal + \")\"); foreach (PhotoSubjectRecord s in photo.subjects) Debug.Log(s.subject + \" doing \" + s.behaviour + \", \" + Mathf.RoundToInt(s.visible * 100f) + \"% visible\"); }; A Photo is the saved record: id , source , judged , score , topScore , medal , passed , mainSubj"},{"p":"Scripting","h":"Change a result","u":"scripting.html#change-a-result","x":"void OnEnable() => PhotoEvents.PhotoEvaluated += Adjust; void OnDisable() => PhotoEvents.PhotoEvaluated -= Adjust; void Adjust(Photo photo, PhotoContext context) { // Double points at night. if (photo.GetData(\"time\") != \"night\") return; photo.score = Mathf.Min(photo.topScore, photo.score * 2f); photo.medal = context.Profile.Scoring.MedalFor(photo.Share); }"},{"p":"Scripting","h":"Photos with no camera component","u":"scripting.html#photos-with-no-camera-component","x":"PhotoShot shot = PhotoShoot.Take(anyCamera, profile, PhotoSource.Script, ready: s => Debug.Log(\"Picture is in\")); PhotoShoot.Take runs the whole pipeline for any Unity Camera: capture, recognise, judge, keep or discard, save. Decide yourself with PhotoShoot.Keep(shot) and PhotoShoot.Discard(shot) when the profile's keep rule is Player Chooses."},{"p":"Scripting","h":"Only recognition, no photo","u":"scripting.html#only-recognition-no-photo","x":"Ask what a camera can see without taking anything, for AI, tutorials or your own viewfinder: var view = PhotoView.From(Camera.main, profile); SubjectSighting sighting = PhotoRecognizer.Look(view, profile.recognition, subject); if (sighting != null && sighting.Recognised) Debug.Log(\"In view, \" + sighting.Visible + \" visible\");"},{"p":"Scripting","h":"Subjects","u":"scripting.html#subjects","x":"var subject = GetComponent<PhotoSubject>(); subject.SetBehaviour(\"eat\"); // doing only this subject.BeginBehaviour(\"glow\"); // doing this as well subject.EndBehaviour(\"glow\"); subject.Photographable = false; // hide from the camera subject.SetData(\"mood\", \"angry\"); // copied into photos of it SubjectRegistry.All lists every enabled subject; SubjectRegistry.Find(id) finds one by its definition key."},{"p":"Scripting","h":"Requests, journal and album","u":"scripting.html#requests-journal-and-album","x":"PhotoRequests.Offer(request); PhotoRequests.Withdraw(request); bool done = PhotoJournal.Main.IsCompleted(request.Id); bool found = PhotoJournal.Main.IsDiscovered(\"zebra\"); JournalEntry entry = PhotoJournal.Main.Entry(\"zebra\"); foreach (Photo photo in PhotoAlbum.Main.Photos) { } PhotoAlbum.Main.LoadThumbnail(photo, texture => image.texture = texture); PhotoAlbum.Main.SetFavourite(photo, true); PhotoAlbum.Main.Remove(photo); string copy = PhotoAlbum.Main.Export(photo); Textures handed over by the album belong to it. Do not destroy them, and ask again when a screen reopens: the album lets go of p"},{"p":"Scripting","h":"Events","u":"scripting.html#events","x":"PhotoEvents (static, for the whole game): CameraOpened , CameraClosed , CaptureRequested , PhotoEvaluated , PhotoAccepted , PhotoRejected , PhotoSaved , SubjectDiscovered , BehaviourDiscovered , NewBestShot , RequestCompleted . Remember to unsubscribe in OnDisable ."},{"p":"Scripting","h":"Extending","u":"scripting.html#extending","x":"To change Write How photos are judged A CustomPhotoScorer asset. See Scoring . A request rule A CustomPhotoRule asset. See Photo requests . Where photos are stored An IPhotoStore , opened with PhotoAlbum.Open(store) . Where input comes from An IPhotoInput , set with PhotoInput.Use(input) . Lens effects An IPhotoEffects , for a custom render pipeline. Words PhotoText.Translate , PhotoText.Set , or a Text Table asset."},{"p":"Scripting","h":"Editor","u":"scripting.html#editor","x":"Tools > Photoworthy > Check Scene For Problems uses the shared Diagnostics window, which lists the checks of every Rare Geometry product in the project."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"Troubleshooting and questions","u":"troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting-and-questions","x":"Start with Tools > Photoworthy > Check Scene For Problems . It finds most setup mistakes and fixes many in one click. For \"why this score?\", use Tools > Photoworthy > Score Inspector ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"Nothing happens when I press C","u":"troubleshooting.html#nothing-happens-when-i-press-c","x":"Is there a Photo Camera in the scene, and does it have a Game Camera (or is your camera tagged MainCamera)? Is the handheld camera enabled in the profile, and is Built In Input on under Keys? With only the Input System active, the kit reads the same keys through it. If your project has neither input system active, call OpenHandheld from your own code. A project made from Unity's 3D (HDRP) template is set to the Input System without having the package, so no keys reach any game in it, and Unity's own UI code logs an error every frame. The Console and the Photo Studio's Check tab say so (\"No key"},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"The selfie shows no character","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-selfie-shows-no-character","x":"In a first-person game the character model is hidden or does not exist. List the model under Shown In Selfie on the Photo Camera, and set Selfie Focus to a transform at its face. If the face fills the picture, raise Arm Length in the profile under Handheld > Selfie; Face Side moves the face to one side."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"My subject is not recognised","u":"troubleshooting.html#my-subject-is-not-recognised","x":"Open the Score Inspector and press Judge Game Camera. The subject is listed under Nearby but not counted with the reason: Reason Fix Too far Raise Max Distance in the profile's Recognition, or on the subject. Too small Zoom in, or lower Min Picture Share. Cut off More of it must be inside the picture. Lower Min In Frame to be more forgiving. Hidden Something with a collider is in the way. Take see-through things (glass, leaves, trigger volumes are already ignored) off the Hiding Layers. Not photographable The subject's Photographable box is off, or its object is disabled. A subject with no ren"},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"The whole subject is in view but it says \"Partly hidden\"","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-whole-subject-is-in-view-but-it-says-partly-hidden","x":"The check casts lines to points over the subject's bounds. For an odd shape (a tree with a thin trunk, an arch) some of those points sit in empty air behind other things. Switch the subject's Anchor Mode to Custom and place a few points on the parts that matter."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"The score seems wrong for my kind of subject","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-score-seems-wrong-for-my-kind-of-subject","x":"Use a scoring preset that suits it (Landmark for buildings, Small Object for clues) and give it to the subject asset as its Scoring Override. Switch off criteria that do not apply: the others share the points."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"The photo mode has no blur, or the filters do nothing","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-photo-mode-has-no-blur-or-the-filters-do-nothing","x":"URP and HDRP : post processing must be on for the game camera (Rendering > Post Processing on the camera in URP). Built-in : the kit adds its own image effect to the camera while a screen is open. A second camera drawn on top of the game camera can cover it."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"The flash does nothing","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-flash-does-nothing","x":"It is day. A flash adds little to a sunlit scene, the same as a real one. Press N in the demos for night. Auto never fires : Auto only fires when the view is darker than Dark Below (profile, Handheld > Flash). Set the mode to On to check the light itself. Unlit materials are not lit by any light. The flash lights what your own lights light. URP : the flash is an extra (additional) light. In the URP Asset, Additional Lights must not be Disabled. HDRP measures light in candela: raise Brightness Hdrp until it suits your scene's exposure."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"My own HUD shows in photos","u":"troubleshooting.html#my-own-hud-shows-in-photos","x":"Photos are a fresh render of the game camera, so screen-space overlay UI never appears. UI on a camera-space or world-space canvas does: list those objects under Storage > Hide In Pictures."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"Photos are too large, or saving stutters","u":"troubleshooting.html#photos-are-too-large-or-saving-stutters","x":"JPG at 1080 is right for almost every game. PNG files are several times larger. Reading the picture back and writing the file run off the main thread; if a very large size with Smooth Edges still hitches on a weak device, lower the Size."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"The album is empty after restarting","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-album-is-empty-after-restarting","x":"Is Save To Disk on in the profile's Storage? Is the Storage Folder the same as last time (a different folder per save slot means a different album)?"},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"Where are the photos?","u":"troubleshooting.html#where-are-the-photos","x":"Application.persistentDataPath , in the profile's Storage Folder. On Windows that is under %USERPROFILE%\\AppData\\LocalLow\\<company>\\<product>\\ ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"Can I use my own UI and still use the album and journal data?","u":"troubleshooting.html#can-i-use-my-own-ui-and-still-use-the-album-and-journal-data","x":"Yes. Switch off the screens you replace; the data, events and textures are all public. See Fit it into your game ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"Does it need the Post Processing package, Cinemachine or TextMeshPro?","u":"troubleshooting.html#does-it-need-the-post-processing-package-cinemachine-or-textmeshpro","x":"No. There are no package dependencies. URP, HDRP and the Input System are used when present."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"Does it work in 2D or with an orthographic camera?","u":"troubleshooting.html#does-it-work-in-2d-or-with-an-orthographic-camera","x":"Recognition works with orthographic cameras and uses 3D colliders for the hidden check. For a 2D game, switch Check Hidden off (or put 3D colliders on what should hide things)."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"Can a photo be judged without making a picture?","u":"troubleshooting.html#can-a-photo-be-judged-without-making-a-picture","x":"Yes. Switch Make Pictures off. The record of what was photographed is still made, requests still complete and the journal still fills."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"Can players cheat by pausing in the photo mode?","u":"troubleshooting.html#can-players-cheat-by-pausing-in-the-photo-mode","x":"By default photo mode photos do not count for progress, exactly for this reason. Switch Counts For Progress on in the profile's Photo Mode if your game wants them to."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"Does anything run when the camera is down?","u":"troubleshooting.html#does-anything-run-when-the-camera-is-down","x":"No. Subjects only register themselves when enabled; the viewfinder's checks run only while a camera screen is open."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and questions","h":"How do I remove the demos?","u":"troubleshooting.html#how-do-i-remove-the-demos","x":"Delete the Samples folder."},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"support.html#support","x":"Need help? Every message gets an answer. In Unity, Tools > Photoworthy > Help and Support lists every support address (Discord, bug reports, email) with Copy buttons, and Copy system info copies the Photoworthy version, Unity version, render pipeline and platform. Paste it in your message. Discord: https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu (ask in #help) Report a bug: https://github.com/raregeometry/Support/issues Email: raregeometrysupport@gmail.com Website: https://raregeometry.github.io/photoworthy/ Enjoying Photoworthy? A short review on the Asset Store helps a lot."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html#changelog","x":"All notable changes to Photoworthy. Format: Keep a Changelog. Versions: Semantic Versioning."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0] - Unreleased","u":"changelog.html#100---unreleased","x":""},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Added","u":"changelog.html#added","x":"Photo Camera: a handheld camera and a photo mode on one component, borrowing the game's own camera. Photo Subjects with automatic or custom points, details, behaviours, tags, places and their own data. Recognition at the shutter: in frame, picture share, hidden behind colliders, facing, with a reason when a subject does not count. Scoring: six criteria with points and reasons, medals, rarity tiers, eight presets, custom scorers, or off. Photo requests from 18 kinds of rule, with AND and OR lists and player words. Journal of discoveries, behaviours and best shots; album of picture files with fa"}];
